1. Overview
This room moves from the basics to the administrative tools used to inspect and control a Windows system: System Configuration, UAC settings, Computer Management, System Information, Resource Monitor, the Command Prompt and the Registry Editor.
2. Learning objectives
Use msconfig to see boot and startup settings.
Understand UAC levels.
Use Computer Management, System Information and Resource Monitor.
Run basic Command Prompt commands.
Explain what the registry stores.
3. Topics covered
Tool
Command
What it shows
Security use
System Configuration
msconfig
General, boot, services and tools tabs.
Check which services and programs start with Windows.
UAC settings
UserAccountControlSettings
The notification level for admin actions.
Weak settings make silent elevation easier.
Computer Management
compmgmt.msc
Task Scheduler, Event Viewer, shared folders, local users and groups, Device Manager, Disk Management and Services.
Review scheduled tasks, shares, accounts and logs in one place.
System Information
msinfo32
Detailed hardware and software summary.
Collect host details during an investigation.
Resource Monitor
resmon
Live CPU, memory, disk and network use per process.
Find processes with strange network activity.
Command Prompt
cmd
Text commands such as hostname, ipconfig, netstat, net user, tasklist.
Quick checks and scripting.
Registry Editor
regedit
The registry database (hives such as HKLM and HKCU).
Check startup keys and settings changed by malware.


5. Key takeaways
These tools are the Windows analyst's everyday toolkit.
Startup items, scheduled tasks and registry Run keys are common places for persistence.
Event Viewer inside Computer Management leads directly into the Event ID work of Week 2.










